Why We Used an Inline IAM Policy Instead of a Managed Policy :
Recently, during a production migration activity in Amazon Web Services, our team had to provide temporary access to an operations engineer for handling non-business-hour EC2 shutdown activities to reduce infra costs after deployment windows.
Requirement was very specific:
✅ User should only stop EC2 instances
✅ Only in production account
✅ No start/reboot/terminate permissions
✅ Access should not be reusable for other users
✅ Permission must automatically disappear once the user is removed
Instead of creating a reusable managed policy, we implemented an Inline IAM Policy directly on the IAM user.
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowOnlyStopInstances",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "ec2:StopInstances",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:ec2:ap-south-1:123456789012:instance/*"
    }
  ]
}
💡 Why Inline Policy here?
Because inline policies maintain a strict one-to-one relationship with the IAM entity.
This helped us:
Avoid accidental permission reuse
Maintain least privilege access
Improve audit compliance
Simplify temporary access cleanup
Reduce operational risk in production
One small IAM design decision can significantly improve cloud security posture.
